<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/calculator.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="707" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/">Guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Reading Your Xactimate Estimate: A Michigan Homeowner Guide</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Guide · 9 min read · October 20, 2026</p><h1>Reading Your Xactimate Estimate: A Michigan Homeowner Guide</h1><p class="ph-lede">A practical walkthrough of the estimate your insurer sent: line items, unit prices, labor minimums, overhead and profit, and depreciation.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Why your estimate looks the way it does</h2>
<p>If your insurer sent a long, dense estimate full of codes and abbreviations, it was probably written in Xactimate. Xactimate is estimating software used by most insurers, many adjusters and a lot of contractors. It builds an estimate line by line, pulling prices from regional price lists that are updated regularly. Understanding how it works is the first step in spotting what’s missing or underpriced. This guide covers the basics in plain terms.</p>
<h2>The structure of an Xactimate estimate</h2>
<p>Most estimates follow the same layout:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Header:</strong> claim number, insured, date of loss, price list used and the date of that price list.</li>
<li><strong>Room or area sections:</strong> each room, roof slope or elevation with its measurements.</li>
<li><strong>Line items:</strong> each task, with quantity, unit, unit price, tax, replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value.</li>
<li><strong>Summary:</strong> totals by coverage, overhead and profit if included, deductible and net payment.</li>
<li><strong>Recap by trade:</strong> totals grouped by roofing, drywall, painting, flooring and so on.</li>
</ul>
<p>Check the price list code in the header first. It should match your area and a recent month. A price list from an older period or a distant region can understate costs.</p>
<h2>Line items: quantity times unit price</h2>
<p>Every line multiplies a quantity by a unit price. Roofing is usually priced per square (100 square feet), drywall and paint per square foot, trim per linear foot and fixtures per each. Three questions apply to every line:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Is the quantity right?</strong> Compare the measured square footage, roof squares or linear feet against your own measurements or a contractor’s.</li>
<li><strong>Is the item right?</strong> “Remove and replace” is different from “detach and reset,” and a lower-grade material code can quietly cut the price.</li>
<li><strong>Is anything missing?</strong> Items like drip edge, starter strip, ice and water shield, permits, content manipulation and final cleaning are often left out.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Unit pricing and what it includes</h2>
<p>Each unit price usually combines material, labor and equipment. Prices reflect averages for the region and the price list date. They don’t always reflect real-world conditions such as a steep roof, a three-story Victorian in Heritage Hill, limited access on a lake cottage or a surge in demand after a major storm. When local contractors consistently bid well above the line price, that gap is worth documenting with written bids.</p>
<p>Watch for steep and high charges on roofs. Pitches above certain thresholds and roofs more than one story up typically carry additional line items. Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/roof-squares-pitch-calculator/">roof squares and pitch calculator</a> helps check whether the measurements and pitch make sense.</p>
<h2>Labor minimums</h2>
<p>Small jobs often cost more per unit because a tradesperson still has to show up, set up and clean up. Xactimate handles this with labor minimum line items for trades such as electrical, plumbing, HVAC and drywall. If an estimate includes a tiny amount of work in a trade, say replacing one outlet or patching a small area of drywall, but no labor minimum, the price likely won’t cover a real visit. Missing labor minimums are a common, easy-to-fix gap.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Small lines add up</b>A missing labor minimum, a forgotten detach-and-reset or an uncounted second coat of paint each seem minor. Across a whole house, they can add up to a meaningful share of the claim.</div>
<h2>Overhead and profit</h2>
<p>Overhead and profit, often written O&amp;P, is a general contractor’s charge for running the job and earning a margin, commonly shown as 10% overhead plus 10% profit. Insurers often include it when the work involves several trades that need coordination, and sometimes leave it out on simpler claims. Whether O&amp;P is owed depends on the policy and the facts, but its absence on a multi-trade loss, such as a fire or a large water claim involving drywall, flooring, electrical and painting, is worth questioning.</p>
<h2>Depreciation</h2>
<p>Each line may show depreciation, which reduces replacement cost to actual cash value. Check the age and life expectancy used for each material, and look for depreciation applied to labor, which some estimates include. Under many replacement cost policies, depreciation is recoverable once repairs are completed. Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV, ACV and holdback calculator</a> shows how that works.</p>
<h2>An illustrative comparison</h2>
<p>In an illustrative example, an insurer’s estimate for a hail-damaged roof in Ann Arbor lists 24 squares at a standard unit price with no drip edge, no ice and water shield, no steep charge and no O&amp;P, for a total of $13,200. A contractor measuring the same roof finds 27 squares including waste, a 9/12 pitch that triggers steep charges, and code-required ice and water shield at the eaves. With those items and O&amp;P for coordinating gutter and siding work, the estimate reaches $21,600. Neither number is automatically right, but the gap is specific, documented and the kind of thing appraisal resolves.</p>
<h2>How to respond to the estimate</h2>
<ul>
<li>Mark each disputed line with the reason: quantity, item, price or missing.</li>
<li>Attach contractor bids, photos and measurements that support your position.</li>
<li>Send a written request for a revised estimate and keep copies.</li>
<li>If the gap remains and the claim is covered, consider appraisal.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/insurance-estimate-second-opinion/">estimate second opinion</a> service reviews insurer estimates line by line and tells you where the real gaps are. If you decide appraisal is the next step, our <a href="/michigan-appraisal-process/">appraisal process guide</a> explains what comes next.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/should-you-demand-appraisal/"><small>Guide</small><span>Should You Demand Appraisal? A Michigan Decision Checklist</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/michigan-appraisal-clause-explained/"><small>Guide</small><span>The Michigan Appraisal Clause Explained in Plain English</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/appraiser-public-adjuster-or-attorney/"><small>Guide</small><span>Appraiser, Public Adjuster or Attorney: Who Fits Your Claim?</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article>
